<BlogPostLayout post={post} lang={lang} faq={faq} references={references}>

  <p class="lead">
    Few herbs are as iconic as <strong>Ginseng</strong>. The name comes from the Chinese <em>rénshēn</em> — literally "man-root" — and the plant has been treasured across East Asia for over <strong>2,000 years</strong> as a tonic for energy, longevity and balance. Today, Panax ginseng is one of the world's most studied adaptogens, with research backing its traditional reputation for <strong>stamina, immune support, stress resilience and mental performance</strong>. This guide explains the different types, the science, and how to use Ginseng safely.
  </p>

  <div class="takeaways" role="region" aria-label="Key takeaways">
    <h2>Key takeaways</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Panax ginseng</strong> (Korean/Asian ginseng) is the most studied true ginseng — used for over 2,000 years.</li>
      <li>Its active compounds — called <strong>ginsenosides</strong> — are unique to the Ginseng family and account for most of its effects.</li>
      <li>Ginseng is classified as an <strong>adaptogen</strong>: it helps the body adapt to physical, mental and environmental stress.</li>
      <li>Strongest traditional and clinical uses: <strong>fatigue, stamina, immune support, mental focus, stress resilience, blood-sugar balance, and sexual vitality</strong>.</li>
      <li>Typical adult dose: <strong>200–400 mg/day</strong> of standardised root extract, often in 8–12 week cycles.</li>
      <li>Avoid Ginseng if you are pregnant or breastfeeding, have uncontrolled high blood pressure, take blood thinners, or are about to have surgery.</li>
      <li>Dynapharm offers Ginseng in <strong>Panax Ginseng Capsules</strong>, <strong>Instant Ginseng Honey Ginger</strong>, and <strong>Ginkgo Ginseng Coffee</strong>.</li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <h2 id="what-is-ginseng">What is Ginseng?</h2>
  <p>
    "Ginseng" is the common name for several plants in the <em>Panax</em> genus — and only true Panax species count as real ginseng. The most important are:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Panax ginseng</strong> — Asian ginseng, also called Korean ginseng. The most studied; traditionally considered "warming" and energising.</li>
    <li><strong>Panax quinquefolius</strong> — American ginseng. Traditionally considered "cooling" and calming.</li>
    <li><strong>Panax notoginseng</strong> — used in Chinese medicine, especially for circulation.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    What is sometimes sold as "Siberian ginseng" (<em>Eleutherococcus senticosus</em>) is <strong>not actually ginseng</strong> — it is a different plant family with different active compounds. Throughout this article, "Ginseng" refers specifically to <strong>Panax ginseng</strong>, unless otherwise noted.
  </p>

  <figure>
    <Image src={inlineImg1} alt="Fresh whole Panax ginseng roots arranged on a natural surface — a traditional Asian medicinal herb" widths={[640, 1024, 1280]} sizes="(min-width: 768px) 720px, 100vw" loading="lazy" />
    <figcaption>The root of Panax ginseng — the source of the active compounds called ginsenosides — is the part used in nearly all supplements.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <h2 id="history">A short history of Ginseng</h2>
  <p>
    Ginseng appears in some of the oldest medical texts in the world. The Chinese <em>Shennong Bencao Jing</em>, a herbal classic dating back roughly two thousand years, describes Panax ginseng as a tonic that <strong>"calms the spirit, brightens the eyes, and prolongs life."</strong> Wild ginseng was once so prized that it was harvested for emperors only, and is still considered the most valuable form today.
  </p>
  <p>
    Modern interest in Ginseng's pharmacology began in the mid-20th century, when Russian and Korean researchers introduced the concept of <strong>adaptogens</strong> — natural substances that help organisms adapt to stress without disrupting normal physiology. Panax ginseng quickly became the prototype adaptogen.
  </p>

  <h2 id="active-compounds">What is in Ginseng? The active compounds</h2>
  <p>
    Ginseng's effects are attributed mainly to a family of compounds called <strong>ginsenosides</strong> (or panaxosides) — natural saponins found almost exclusively in the Panax genus. Over <strong>40 different ginsenosides</strong> have been identified, and different types are thought to produce different effects:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Rg1, Rb1 and Rg3</strong> — among the most studied; linked to mental performance, immune support and antioxidant activity.</li>
    <li>Other compounds in Ginseng include polysaccharides, peptides, essential oils and trace minerals — all of which contribute to its overall profile.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Quality matters: a good Ginseng supplement specifies its <strong>ginsenoside content</strong> (often 3–8% standardised) and uses roots aged at least 4–6 years, when ginsenoside levels peak.
  </p>

  <h2 id="benefits">Health benefits of Ginseng</h2>

  <h3>1. May help reduce fatigue and support natural energy</h3>
  <p>
    The most consistent finding in modern Ginseng research is its potential to <strong>reduce fatigue</strong>, especially mental and stress-related fatigue. Several controlled studies have shown standardised extracts can improve subjective energy, motivation and stamina compared to placebo, particularly in people who feel chronically tired.
  </p>

  <h3>2. May support immune function</h3>
  <p>
    Ginseng has a long traditional reputation for "strengthening" the immune system, especially during the cold season. Modern research suggests certain ginsenosides and polysaccharides may help support healthy immune cell activity, although clinical evidence is still evolving.
  </p>

  <h3>3. May enhance mental performance and focus</h3>
  <p>
    Several human trials suggest Panax ginseng may modestly improve <strong>working memory, calmness and mental arithmetic</strong> performance, particularly in stressful or cognitively demanding situations. Combined with <strong>Ginkgo Biloba</strong>, the effect on alertness can be more pronounced — which is one reason the two herbs are so often paired.
  </p>

  <h3>4. May help the body adapt to stress</h3>
  <p>
    As an adaptogen, Ginseng is thought to help <strong>balance the body's stress response</strong>, particularly the activity of the hypothalamic–pituitary–adrenal (HPA) axis. People taking it regularly often report a calmer, steadier feeling under pressure.
  </p>

  <h3>5. May support healthy blood-sugar balance</h3>
  <p>
    Ginseng has been studied for its potential to support healthy blood-sugar levels in adults, with several small trials suggesting modest improvements after meals. <strong>People with diabetes should not self-prescribe</strong> Ginseng — it can interact with medication and should only be added under medical guidance.
  </p>

  <h3>6. Antioxidant and cellular support</h3>
  <p>
    Ginsenosides have demonstrated <strong>antioxidant activity</strong> in laboratory studies, helping neutralise free radicals that contribute to cellular ageing. This is part of why Ginseng has been associated with longevity in traditional medicine.
  </p>

  <h3>7. May support male sexual vitality</h3>
  <p>
    Panax ginseng has traditional and modern reputations as a tonic for male vitality. Several clinical trials report improvements in erectile function and libido in men taking standardised Ginseng extract for several weeks. Effects are usually modest but consistent across studies.
  </p>

