    <figcaption>In Asian tradition, Ginseng is often combined with other vitality herbs such as goji berries, jujube and ginger.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <h2 id="safety">Safety, side effects and interactions</h2>
  <p>
    Ginseng is generally well tolerated by healthy adults. When side effects occur, they are usually mild and may include trouble sleeping (especially if taken in the evening), headaches, restlessness, or digestive discomfort. Reducing the dose or stopping the supplement typically resolves these.
  </p>
  <h3>When to avoid Ginseng</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Pregnancy and breastfeeding</strong> — safety has not been established.</li>
    <li><strong>Uncontrolled high blood pressure</strong> — Ginseng may raise blood pressure in some people.</li>
    <li><strong>Hormone-sensitive conditions</strong> — Ginseng may have mild oestrogen-like effects.</li>
    <li><strong>Diabetes medication</strong> — risk of low blood sugar without medical supervision.</li>
    <li><strong>Blood thinners</strong> (warfarin, aspirin, clopidogrel) — Ginseng may affect clotting.</li>
    <li><strong>Stimulant medications</strong> or MAO inhibitors — possible interactions.</li>
    <li>If you are scheduled for <strong>surgery within 2 weeks</strong>.</li>
  </ul>

